entries.answer:
Yes — the right strategy is to treat search as a **baseline UIKit feature** and treat newer visual systems as optional enhancements layered on top. Apple’s WWDC25 guidance says standard controls automatically adopt the newer design when apps are rebuilt with newer tools and run on newer systems, while the older look remains unchanged in older build/runtime combinations, so compatibility should come from using standard components first and only adding modern styling conditionally.[1]

## Availability model

For this kind of app, `UISearchController` should be the foundation because UIKit already supports placing its search interface in the navigation bar through `navigationItem.searchController`.  That makes your compatibility story much better than building a custom search surface tied to newer system materials or navigation behaviors.[2][3][1]

Your design principle is also correct: do not make Liquid Glass or other newer appearance treatments a functional dependency. Apple’s WWDC25 session describes the new design as something standard controls can adopt automatically on newer releases, which is exactly why older systems should keep using ordinary UIKit presentations rather than trying to emulate newer effects manually.[1]

## UIKit fixes

The sample you pasted is directionally right, but a few UIKit details should be corrected for it to compile and behave properly. `UISearchResultsUpdating` uses the method `updateSearchResults(for:)`, not `updateSearchResultsFor(searchController:)`, and Apple’s documentation and common UIKit examples describe that as the protocol method you implement to react to search text changes.[4][5][3]

There are also two Swift issues in the snippet: `private val resultsController` should be `private let resultsController`, and `searchController?.searchBar` should not use optional chaining if `searchController` is a non-optional property. Those are code-level fixes rather than platform rules, but they matter because the current example would fail before you even reach the availability logic.

## Safer structure

A stronger version is to always create one `UISearchController`, always assign its updater, and then vary only its placement. On systems where navigation-bar integration is appropriate, set `navigationItem.searchController = searchController`; when you need a legacy fallback layout, place `searchController.searchBar` directly in your view hierarchy or a header container instead.[3][2]

```swift
final class SearchContainerViewController: UIViewController {
    private let resultsController = ResultsViewController()

    private lazy var searchController: UISearchController = {
        let sc = UISearchController(searchResultsController: resultsController)
        sc.searchResultsUpdater = self
        sc.searchBar.delegate = self
        sc.obscuresBackgroundDuringPresentation = false
        sc.searchBar.placeholder = "Search Landmarks"
        return sc
    }()

    private let legacySearchContainer = UIView()

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        definesPresentationContext = true
        setupSearch()
    }

    private func setupSearch() {
        if #available(iOS 26.0, *) {
            navigationItem.searchController = searchController
            navigationItem.hidesSearchBarWhenScrolling = false
        } else {
            legacySearchContainer.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
            searchController.searchBar.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false

            view.addSubview(legacySearchContainer)
            legacySearchContainer.addSubview(searchController.searchBar)

            NSLayoutConstraint.activate([
                legacySearchContainer.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.safeAreaLayoutGuide.topAnchor),
                legacySearchContainer.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.leadingAnchor),
                legacySearchContainer.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: view.trailingAnchor),

                searchController.searchBar.topAnchor.constraint(equalTo: legacySearchContainer.topAnchor),
                searchController.searchBar.leadingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: legacySearchContainer.leadingAnchor),
                searchController.searchBar.trailingAnchor.constraint(equalTo: legacySearchContainer.trailingAnchor),
                searchController.searchBar.bottomAnchor.constraint(equalTo: legacySearchContainer.bottomAnchor)
            ])
        }
    }
}

extension SearchContainerViewController: UISearchResultsUpdating, UISearchBarDelegate {
    func updateSearchResults(for searchController: UISearchController) {
        let text = searchController.searchBar.text ?? ""
        resultsController.filterContent(for: text)
    }
}
```

## Why this degrades well

This pattern works because the search behavior is owned by `UISearchController`, while only the presentation changes by OS version. Apple documents the navigation-item integration point for the controller, and the results-updating protocol is explicitly meant to refresh filtered content from text entered in the search bar.[2][4]

It also aligns with Apple’s newer design rollout: standard controls can gain the modern system treatment automatically on newer releases, but you do not need separate search logic for that to happen.  In practice, that means your app stays usable on an iPhone X–class deployment target while still looking appropriately modern on newer systems.[2][1]

## Navigation appearance

For navigation bars, keep older systems explicit and newer systems lighter-touch. Because search integration depends on the navigation bar on supported setups, you should configure navigation appearance on older targets for consistency, but avoid hardcoding visual treatments that fight the system-rendered materials on newer releases where standard controls pick up the newer design language automatically.[1][2]

The practical takeaway is simple: use stock UIKit search APIs everywhere, gate only presentation and styling behind `#available`, and never let a newer appearance API become required for the search workflow itself. That gives you one search implementation, one filtering pipeline, and two presentation paths instead of two separate features.[4][2][1]

